<h2>Step 2: Build your evidence</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Start with the <a href="/reference/case-studies/">Case Study Library</a> card for your event.</li>
  <li>Use at least three sources. At least one must be data: a measured record, a map, or an official event report. A news article is useful but it is not data.</li>
  <li>Find at least one source yourself.</li>
  <li>Write one or two lines of <a href="/foundations/opvl-framework/">OPVL</a> for each source on a separate source page.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Step 3: Make your poster</h2>
<p>One poster, A3 or A2, printed or digital. It must have three parts.</p>
<p>A visitor should be able to understand your main claim from 2 meters away in under 30 seconds. Put details lower down and in smaller text.</p>
<p><strong>Part 1: Key terms.</strong> Choose the six to eight terms from the unit vocabulary list that someone needs in order to understand your event. Use each one correctly in your explanation. Do not make a glossary box: the terms should appear where they are needed.</p>
<p><strong>Part 2: Cause and effect.</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li>Where: a map showing the event and the plates involved.</li>
  <li>Cause: a diagram of what the plates were doing, drawn by you. Show the plates, the direction each one moves, and where the energy or magma came from.</li>
  <li>Effect: what happened at the surface and to people. Explain why this setting produced this hazard and not a different one.</li>
  <li>Include at least two pieces of evidence, each with a short citation next to it.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Part 3: Solutions.</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li>What did people change after this event? Name one specific change (a building code, a warning system, an evacuation plan, a land-use rule).</li>
  <li>Did it work? Give evidence if you can find it.</li>
  <li>What would you recommend now, and what is one thing we still do not know?</li>
</ul>
<p>Give your poster a title that is a claim, not a topic. "The warning came, but the wall was too low" is a claim. "The 2011 Japan Earthquake" is a topic.</p>

<div class="callout extend">
  <div class="callout-title">Stretch: find a feedback loop</div>
  <p>For a higher Thinking and Transfer score, show one feedback loop connected to your event. Use the Unit 1 conventions: components in boxes, a verb on every arrow, the loop comes back to where it started, and the sign test is shown. Some places to look:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>After a disaster, what happens to building rules, and what happens to people's attention as the years pass?</li>
    <li>When a sea wall is built, what happens to where people choose to live?</li>
    <li>Why do people keep farming on the slopes of active volcanoes?</li>
    <li>What happens to stress on a fault between one earthquake and the next?</li>
  </ul>
  <p>You may decide that what you found is a chain and not a loop. If you can show why it does not close, that counts.</p>
</div>

<h2>Step 4: Defend your poster</h2>
<p>Prepare using <a href="/foundations/defending-your-work/">Defending Your Work in This Class</a>.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Expect all five question types: comprehension check, evidence challenge, position challenge, a hard or trap question, and clarification.</li>
  <li>Prepare an "I don't know, but here's how I'd find out" answer for three questions you might get.</li>
  <li>You may hold one index card. You may ask for a question to be said another way.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Using AI</h2>
<p>You may use AI to find sources, check your reasoning, practice your defense and edit your wording.</p>
<p>You may not use AI to draw or generate your plate boundary diagram. The diagram is your model, and it is what is being assessed.</p>
<p>Check any number AI gives you against your card or a source. Each card lists what AI often gets wrong about that event.</p>
<p>Document every AI use with the <a href="/student-hub/ai-documentation-template/">AI Documentation Template</a> from Unit 0. If you used AI and the documentation is missing, your work will be returned as incomplete and your score held until it is submitted.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">A note on these events</div>
  <p>These are real disasters in which many people died, and some happened within living memory in this region. Write and speak about them with respect. Use numbers accurately, and do not use images of injured people or victims.</p>
</div>

<h2>Before you submit</h2>
<ul>
  <li>My title is a claim.</li>
  <li>Part 1: six to eight key terms, used correctly where they are needed.</li>
  <li>Part 2: a map, my own diagram of the plates, and an explanation of why this hazard happened here.</li>
  <li>Part 3: one specific change, whether it worked, my recommendation, one unknown.</li>
  <li>At least three sources, one of them data, one I found myself.</li>
  <li>Source page with OPVL.</li>
  <li>AI Documentation, if I used AI.</li>
  <li>Three "I don't know, but here's how I'd find out" answers prepared.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Rubric</h2>
<p>Each strand is scored on the department 8-point scale and reported separately. The strands are not added together. The poster and the questions are scored together: the answers can raise or lower a band set by the poster.</p>

<h3>Knowledge and Understanding (K/U)</h3>
<p><em>The student recalls facts, terms and definitions while also explaining concepts, ideas, processes, themes, and the like. The student learns and performs skills related to their studies.</em></p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>7-8 Exemplary</th><th>5-6 Proficient</th><th>3-4 Developing</th><th>1-2 Limited</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>The plate diagram is accurate and complete: the right plates, the right directions of motion, and the source of the energy or magma.</td><td>The diagram is accurate. One element is vague or missing.</td><td>The diagram is partly accurate, or shows the wrong setting.</td><td>The diagram is mostly inaccurate, missing, or not the student's own.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Explains each step from plate motion to hazard accurately, and why this setting produced this hazard and not another.</td><td>Explains most steps accurately.</td><td>Explains some steps. The link from setting to hazard is asserted, not explained.</td><td>Little accurate explanation.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Six to eight key terms are used precisely, where they are needed.</td><td>Most terms are used correctly.</td><td>Terms are listed or defined but not used, or used with errors.</td><td>Terms are largely absent or misused.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Answers comprehension questions clearly and correctly, including on points the student did not prepare.</td><td>Answers most comprehension questions correctly.</td><td>Answers some. Relies on reading the poster.</td><td>Cannot explain the poster's content when asked.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>The vocabulary quiz also informs the K/U band.</p>

<h3>Thinking and Transfer (T/T)</h3>
<p><em>The student plans for learning by formulating questions, generating ideas, and researching. The student thinks about learning critically by analyzing, interpreting, evaluating, and using other metacognitive skills. The student applies knowledge and skills to unfamiliar or new contexts.</em></p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>7-8 Exemplary</th><th>5-6 Proficient</th><th>3-4 Developing</th><th>1-2 Limited</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Uses data, not only news reports. OPVL notes show real evaluation, and at least one Limitation changes how a source is used.</td><td>Uses at least one data source with sound OPVL.</td><td>Presents evidence but does not judge it. OPVL is descriptive.</td><td>Little evidence, or evidence unrelated to the event.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Evaluates whether the change made after the event worked, with evidence, including side effects or limits.</td><td>Names a specific change and gives a reasoned judgment of it.</td><td>Names a change but does not evaluate it.</td><td>No specific change named.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>The recommendation follows from the analysis. States a meaningful unknown and how it could be investigated.</td><td>Gives a reasonable recommendation and states an unknown.</td><td>The recommendation or the unknown is generic ("more research is needed").</td><td>No recommendation and no unknown.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Under questioning, weighs a challenge and revises a position when given a better argument, explaining why.</td><td>Responds to challenges with reasoning.</td><td>Defends by repeating the poster.</td><td>Cannot respond to challenges.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Stretch: a correct feedback loop with the sign test, or a well-argued case that a proposed loop is only a chain, is strong evidence for the 7-8 band in the second and third rows.</p>

